Title: The Resilience and Reinforcement of Group Cohesion in the Face of Economic Inequality
Abstract: Building on evidence that group cohesion drives economic efficiency, we hypothesise income inequality influences efficiency by altering cohesion, with the direction depending on inequality type. Using a novel experimental design with extensive group interactions and a state-of-the-art group cohesion measure, we confirm stronger cohesion is associated with more efficient interactions. Group cohesion is strengthened by inequality between groups, a mechanism previously overlooked. Furthermore, unexpectedly, inequalities within groups do not hinder cohesion. These results suggest interpersonal relationships display resilience to economic disparities, challenging the prevailing argument that inequality must harm cohesion, and undermining a pathway through which it could reduce efficiency.
